{"records":[{"id":"jr96bw6m25","title":"Parsing the role of NSP1 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Fisher et al","description":"SARS-CoV-2 leads to shutoff of cellular protein synthesis and expression of nsp1, a central shutoff factor in coronaviruses, inhibits cellular gene translation.\nWe reveal the multifaceted approach nsp1 uses to shut off cellular protein synthesis and uncover the central role it plays in SARS-CoV-2 pathogenesis, explicitly through blockage of the IFN response.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-03T14:38:47.665Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jr96bw6m25","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jr96bw6m25"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tal","last_name":"Fisher","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Molecular Biology","Virus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ps5pftptkp","title":"SARS-CoV-2 Infection Induces Beta Cell Transdifferentiation","description":"Recent clinical data has suggested a correlation between Coronavirus disease 19 (COVID-19) and diabetes. Here, we describe the detection of SARS-CoV-2 viral antigen in pancreatic beta cells in autopsy samples from COVID-19 patients. Single cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq) and immunostaining from ex vivo infections confirmed that multiple types of pancreatic islet cells are susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, eliciting a cellular stress response and the induction of chemokines.  Upon SARS-CoV-2 infection, beta cells show a decreased expression of insulin and the increased expression of alpha and acinar cell markers, including glucagon and PRSS1/trypsin1, respectively, suggesting cellular transdifferentiation. Trajectory analysis indicated that SARS-CoV-2 induced eIF2 pathway-mediated beta cell transdifferentiation, a phenotype that could be reversed with trans-integrated stress response inhibitor (trans-ISRIB). Altogether, this study demonstrates the first example of SARS-CoV-2 infection causing cell fate change, which provides a new contributing mechanism towards the pathogenesis of COVID-19 patients.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-04T11:20:26.59Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ps5pftptkp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ps5pftptkp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shuibing","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yvgx2sgsf6","title":"Hypoxic and pharmacological activation of HIF inhibits SARS-CoV-2 infection of lung epithelial cells. Wing and Keeley et al","description":"COVID-19, caused by the novel coronavirus SARS-CoV-2, is a global health issue with more than 2 million fatalities to date. Viral replication is shaped by the cellular microenvironment, and one important factor to consider is oxygen tension, in which hypoxia inducible factor (HIF) regulates transcriptional responses to hypoxia. SARS-CoV-2 primarily infects cells of the respiratory tract, entering via its spike glycoprotein binding to angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2). We demonstrate that hypoxia and the HIF prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor Rroxadustat reduce ACE2 expression and inhibit SARS-CoV-2 entry and replication in lung epithelial cells via an HIF-1α-dependent pathway. Hypoxia and Roxadustat inhibit SARS-CoV-2 RNA replication, showing that post-entry steps in the viral life cycle are oxygen sensitive. This study highlights the importance of HIF signaling in regulating multiple aspects of SARS-CoV-2 infection and raises the potential use of HIF prolyl hydroxylase inhibitors in the prevention or treatment of COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-21T07:53:43.611Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yvgx2sgsf6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yvgx2sgsf6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Peter ","last_name":"Wing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Keeley","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yz5srf3sms","title":"Sarbecovirus ORF6 proteins hamper the induction of interferon signaling. Kimura et al","description":"The presence of an ORF6 gene distinguishes sarbecoviruses such as SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 from other betacoronaviruses. Here, we show that ORF6 inhibits the induction of innate immune signaling including upregulation of type I IFN upon viral infection, as well as type I and III IFN signaling. Intriguingly, ORF6 proteins from SARS-CoV-2 lineages are more efficient antagonists of innate immunity than their orthologs from SARS-CoV lineages. Mutational analyses identified residues E46 and Q56 as important determinants of the antagonistic activity of SARS-CoV-2 ORF6. Moreover, we show that the anti-innate immune activity of ORF6 depends on its C-terminal region and ORF6 inhibits the nuclear translocation of IRF3. Finally, we identify naturally occurring frameshift/nonsense mutations that result in an inactivating truncation of ORF6 in approximately 0.2% of SARS-CoV-2 isolates. Altogether, our findings suggest that ORF6 contributes to the poor IFN activation observed in COVID-19 patients.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-12T13:02:54.103Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yz5srf3sms","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yz5srf3sms"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kei","last_name":"Sato","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jgw2mcgb67","title":"SARS-CoV-2 infection of ocular cells from human adult donor eyes and hESC-derived ocular lineages, Eriksen et al","description":"The SARS-CoV-2 pandemic has resulted in an unparalleled disruption of global behavior and a significant loss of life. To minimize SARS-CoV-2 spread, understanding the mechanisms of infection from all possible routes of entry is essential. While aerosol transmission is thought to be the primary route of spread, we assessed the eyes as an alternative point of entry. To this end, we confirmed expression of SARS-CoV-2 entry factors in post-mortem ocular surface tissue and productive viral replication in eye organoid cultures, most notably the limbus. Transcriptional analysis of ex vivo infected ocular surface cells and eye organoids revealed a robust induction of NF-κB in infected cells as well as diminished type-I/III interferon signaling.  Together these data suggest that the eye can be directly infected by SARS-CoV-2 and implicates limbus as a portal for viral entry.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-06T07:26:25.435Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jgw2mcgb67","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jgw2mcgb67"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Timothy","last_name":"Blenkinsop","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r52v2drd9b","title":"Aguilar-Pineda et al. Structural and functional analysis of female sex hormones against SARS-Cov-2 cell entry","description":"This dataset contains all the raw data and codes used for th adquision of the data presented in the manuscript: \"Structural and functional analysis of female sex hormones against SARS-Cov-2 cell entry \" by Aguilar-Pineda et al.\nHere we report new insights into the molecular basis of the interactions between the SARS-CoV-2 spike (S) protein and the human ACE2 receptor. We further report that glycosylation of the ACE2 receptor enhances SARS-CoV-2 infectivity. Importantly estrogens can disrupt glycan-glycan interactions and glycan-protein interactions between the human ACE2 and the SARS-CoV2 thereby blocking its entry into cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-11T13:04:31.536Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r52v2drd9b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r52v2drd9b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jorge Alberto","last_name":"Aguilar Pineda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karin J.","last_name":"Vera López","orcid":"0000-0002-7908-8230"},{"first_name":"Rita M.","last_name":"Nieto-Montesinos","orcid":"0000-0002-5671-4593"},{"first_name":"Gonzalo H. ","last_name":"Davila-Del Carpio","orcid":"0000-0001-7842-9286"},{"first_name":"Badhin","last_name":"Gómez Valdez","orcid":"0000-0001-6539-1207"},{"first_name":"Christian L. ","last_name":"Lino Cardenas","orcid":"0000-0001-5491-4375"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Structural Biology","Estrogen","Molecular Dynamics Study","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"swh2crwhpg","title":"HCQ blocks SARS-CoV-2 entry into the endocytic pathway in mammalian cell culture","description":"HCQ blocks SARS-CoV-2 entry into the endocytic pathway in mammalian cell culture. Communications Biology, 2022.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-07-15T14:27:56.224Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/swh2crwhpg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/swh2crwhpg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zixuan","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Hansen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"57j37g6kdh","title":"High-resolution photocatalytic mapping of SARS-CoV-2 spike interactions on the cell surface. Datta et al","description":"Identifying virus-host interactions on the cell surface can improve our understanding of viral entry and pathogenesis. SARS-CoV-2, the causative agent of the COVID-19 disease, uses ACE2 as a receptor to enter cells. Yet the full repertoire of cell surface proteins that contribute to viral entry is unknown. We developed a photocatalyst-based viral-host protein microenvironment mapping platform (ViraMap) to probe the molecular neighborhood of the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein on the human cell surface. Application of ViraMap to ACE2-expressing cells captured ACE2, the established co-receptor NRP1, and several novel cell surface proteins. We systematically analyzed the relevance of these candidate proteins to SARS-CoV-2 entry by knockdown and overexpression approaches in pseudovirus and authentic infection models and identified PTGFRN and EFNB1 as bona fide viral entry factors. Our results highlight additional host targets that participate in SARS-CoV-2 infection and showcase ViraMap as a powerful platform for defining viral interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-07T06:19:10.123Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/57j37g6kdh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/57j37g6kdh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mohsan","last_name":"Saeed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suprama","last_name":"Datta","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":108342,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":121,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":108342,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":107896,"restricted":55,"embargoed":391},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1895},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":564},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":425},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":365},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":362},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":357},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":291},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":230},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":220}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":105822},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1883},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":167},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":112},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":66},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":63},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":46},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":29},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":445},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":384},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":328},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":269},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":268}]}}